WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.540
The Punisher may have made his debut over
on Daredevil, and both shows share the character

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.750
Karen Page, but Marvel's long-awaited Punisher
series is all Castle.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.320
The Punisher finds himself in the middle of
a government conspiracy that ties back to

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.370
his time running black ops overseas.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.710
Now, Castle has a chance for vengeance.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.470
Bloody, bone-breaking vengeance.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.740
But when the screen finally fades to black,
what actually happened?

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.950
Here's a recap of the ending of The Punisher
and what it all means.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.640
Warning: spoilers and grunting ahead.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.480
(heavy grunting)

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:34.020
Quest for vengeance

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.540
The series opens with Castle tracking down
the rest of the folks he thought were involved

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:41.450
with his family's murder, only to eventually
learn the web of betrayal goes even higher

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:42.820
than he'd suspected.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:47.370
He starts working with David Lieberman a.k.a.
Micro, to unravel the conspiracy, and the

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.750
mission eventually sees them cross paths with
Homeland Security agent Dinah Madani, who's

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:56.320
investigating an operation from Castle's tenure
on the illegal black ops squad.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.470
Through a very tenuous alliance, they finally
realize shady intelligence official William

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.830
Rawlins is really pulling the strings.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:07.260
Rawlins managed Castle's black ops team, and
is trying to clean up the mess—and any lingering

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.430
evidence—by any means necessary.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:13.650
With a new target for revenge, Castle spends
much of the series tracking Rawlins, finally

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:17.150
breaking free after being captured and beating
him to a bloody pulp.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.800
This is the man who pulled the strings to
have Frank's family shot…so let's just say

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.420
Rawlins' comeuppance is as brutal as it is
richly deserved.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.030
A new life

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:31.610
For a show that spends so much time with flying
bullets and bloodied fists, it ends on a surprisingly

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:32.850
quiet moment.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:36.890
After spending the past few years on the run
in hiding, Frank can finally live a life in

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.159
public if he chooses.

00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:42.710
He seems to be coming to grips with that realization
in the show's final scene, as Frank attends

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:46.280
one of the veteran support group meetings
led by his pal Curtis.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.970
Castle talks about what it's like being a
soldier without a mission.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:53.780
Though that obviously works in a broad sense—he's
talking to a room of veterans, after all—Castle

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.930
is also referring to the fact that his quest
for vengeance is over.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:01.479
He's free—and not knowing what comes next
is the scariest thing of all.

00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:02.640
Jigsaw?

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.830
The big surprise in the show's final episode
was the Punisher's decision to actually let

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.890
Billy Russo live following their fight at
the carousel.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:14.670
Instead of finishing him off, Castle decided
to drag his face across a broken mirror, taking

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.540
away Russo's own identity—his looks and
his charm.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.360
"God made me this way for a reason.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.650
I'm just trying to share the wealth."

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:26.780
For comics fans, this didn't come as a surprise—they
already know Russo as the Punisher villain

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:27.780
Jigsaw.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.980
He was a mob hitman in the comics, disfigured
after the Punisher tosses him through a window.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.030
While they don't have as much of a connected
backstory in the comics, making them soldiers

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:39.840
in the same unit served as a way to make a
tangible connection between their origins

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.030
on TV.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:44.650
Series showrunner Steve Lightfoot has teased
they're "only halfway through" with Russo's

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.340
story, and if he returns he'll be a "totally
different guy."

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.320
The Punisher works best when the mission is
personal, and this could certainly fit the

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:52.880
bill.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.720
Micro's happy ending

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.330
Castle's partner Lieberman spent the better
part of a year hiding out in an industrial

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:02.600
basement after faking his death, only able
to see his family via the surveillance cameras

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.250
he planted before he left.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:08.000
He's been forced to watch his loved ones move
on without him, but as Micro and Punisher's

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.490
mission draws closer to its end, Lieberman
is able to reconnect with his family and finally

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:13.490
reveal himself.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:15.650
It's an awkward, but heartfelt, reunion.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:19.370
By the time the series wraps, they seem to
be headed for some form of protective custody

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:22.780
in the wake of the epic conspiracy that unraveled
in Season 1.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:26.050
If nothing else, at least somebody got a happy
ending.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:27.670
Madani survives

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.660
Though Castle metes out justice as well as
anyone, he did require a bit of help from

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:36.380
the other side—specifically through an uneasy
alliance with Homeland Security agent Madani,

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:40.490
who initially needed Castle to testify to
bring down the black ops conspiracy.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:44.300
She protected Castle when Russo shot him in
the stairwell, and once again saved his life

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.250
by taking Castle to her parents' house for
medical care—as opposed to a hospital, where

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.220
he would've been arrested, or worse.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.239
Madani tracked Castle and Russo to their final
showdown at the carousel, and though she was

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:59.050
shot by Russo, she gave Castle the opportunity
to get the drop on Russo and win the fight.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:02.920
Castle got Madani medical attention, and she
repaid his kindness by helping cut the deal

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.450
for him to get a new identity and go free.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:09.519
As the season ends, she still has her job—and
looks to likely move up the Homeland Security

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:12.610
ranks after exposing the conspiracy.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:13.840
What's next?

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.039
More than any other Marvel show on Netflix,
the first season of Punisher truly felt self-contained,

00:04:19.039 --> 00:04:23.229
using its 13 episodes to pick up the final
threads of Castle's quest for revenge and

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:25.150
put a period at the end of that story.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:27.689
It even ended with Castle unsure of what comes
next.

00:04:27.689 --> 00:04:31.319
He doesn't have a mission, but that doesn't
mean he can't find one if Netflix wants a

00:04:31.319 --> 00:04:32.500
second season.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:36.130
As of the making of this video, Lightfoot
says he hasn't heard anything about a potential

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:40.050
second season, but considering every other
Marvel show has scored a renewal, the odds

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:44.770
are decent we'll see Bernthal sport his trademark
skull armor again, and Billy Russo may be

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:45.879
back as well.

00:04:45.879 --> 00:04:50.319
Lightfoot told Entertainment Weekly: "I felt
like we gave Billy a great trajectory of his

00:04:50.319 --> 00:04:52.289
own, which we’re now only halfway through.

00:04:52.289 --> 00:04:54.770
If we come back to him, he’s gonna be totally
different guy."

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:59.289
If and when that renewal order comes, viewers
should brace themselves for the formal introduction

00:04:59.289 --> 00:05:00.379
of Jigsaw.

00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:01.379
Thanks for watching!

